{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794243955,"nonce":"5KOQWF8fznlq3jU2Z4LXMnkbLTM=","title":"Degree-1 N1E periodic tie builds a dense prolongation and densifies every reduction","message":"build_periodic_prolongation_n1e stores P as a dense (n_edges, n_kept) array and flags it is_selection=False. reduce_matrix then has no BCOO P to remap and falls back to the dense P^T A P. The reduced operator reaches the linear solver dense, so lu(backend='pardiso') solves it with a dense LU and PARDISO is never called. Memory grows as n^2: a lowest-order N1E periodic cell of a few hundred thousand edges cannot be built. Degree \u003e= 2 goes through periodic_prolongation and stays sparse. The edge matching also forms a dense (n_secondary, n_main) distance matrix per tie.\n\nReproduction: a 3-D N1E order=1 curl-curl form on a box with u(left) - u(right) and u(front) - u(back); type(reduce_matrix_periodic(fem._periodic, A)) is a dense array, not BCOO.\n\nFix: one nonzero per row (the kept identity, or the chained sign x phase to its main), so P is a signed selection. Build it as BCOO with is_selection=True; _selection_maps/_remap_bcoo already carry the weight and the conjugate. Match edges with a KD-tree.","files":null}]}